The instruction says "Three words to avoid", which is a count of the words in the list. It describes the list and does not limit how many times the agent may do anything, so it states no limit.

The previous unit ("under 10") is a separate line and does not apply here.

{"limits": []}

The instruction says to check "three things" before passing a request on. That "three" counts the checks to run. It describes how many items follow, and it does not cap or require a number of acts in the way a rule limit does. So it works as a count of list items, not a limit.

Still, could "three things" be read as a required minimum count of checks? The checks are listed in the following units, so the number only names the size of the list. It is not a limit.

{"limits": []}

The instruction sets a threshold of 80 euros. Above it, the refund goes to the practice manager and the agent may not handle it. So the rule applies when the refund exceeds 80 euros, which makes it a trigger with comparison gt.

The agent's own allowance is a refund of at most 80 euros. That is only implied, though. The stated number is the range where the rule applies, so I record it as a trigger.

{"limits": [{"limit": 80, "comparison": "gt", "role": "trigger", "unit": "euros (refund amount)"}]}

The instruction implies a cap of four conversations: a fifth must not be opened. The number five appears as "fifth", which is a count of conversations. The rule applies when a fifth conversation would open, i.e. when the number of conversations would exceed four, or reach five. As a trigger: the count of conversations would be at least 5 (or more than 4).

The compliant range is at most four conversations, but the instruction is phrased as a condition ("If a fifth would open"), so the trigger reading fits best. The limit is 5 with comparison ge, role trigger.

{"limits": [{"limit": 5, "comparison": "ge", "role": "trigger", "unit": "conversations"}]}
